WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.109
Ralph Breaks The Internet is bursting with
pop-culture easter eggs and references as

00:00:05.109 --> 00:00:10.000
well as in-jokes poking fun at Disney itself,
but on top of that, Disney also drops in some

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.740
sneaky gags making fun of their own fans!

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:17.210
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video I'm going to reveal 5 ways

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:21.630
the Wreck-It Ralph sequel totally trolls Disney,
Marvel and Pixar fans!

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:26.420
My Funko Pops giveaway is also running on
this video, so hit the bell and tap the Gleam

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.880
link in the video description to enter.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.600
When Vanellope arrives at the fansite Oh My
Disney, she discovers Groot taking part in

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.460
an Ask Me Anything Q&amp;A session, which is amusing
because, of course, all his answers are "I

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:39.460
am Groot".

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:42.840
The first audience question comes from a fan
who has shades of The Simpsons' Comic Book

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:43.840
Guy about him.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.410
And who manages to create some ear-piercing
audio feedback on the microphone in his desperate

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:52.850
quest for an answer to the following question:
"In Captain America: The First Avenger, Red

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:57.429
Skull refers to Yggdrasil, the World Tree,
guardian of wisdom and faith; are we to believe,

00:00:57.429 --> 00:01:00.010
Sir, that you are a descendant of that tree?"

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:04.000
Groot replies in his inimitable fashion and
the Marvel fan thanks him!

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.280
Of course, Disney are poking fun here at comic
con Q&amp;As and all of us Marvel fans who love

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.300
to spend time online connecting all the dots
between their movies and coming up with sometimes

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.320
wild theories about the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.340
By the way, the obsessive Marvel fan in Wreck-It
Ralph 2 is voiced by Jason Mantzoukas whose

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:25.060
character in the film is called Hey Nongman,
which is a catchphrase on the podcast "Comedy

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:26.060
Bang!

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:27.520
Bang!" which features Mantzoukas.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.070
Talk about deep-cut references!

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:34.130
After Disney officially announced the movie's
title as Ralph Breaks The Internet: Wreck-It

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:39.530
Ralph 2 back in March 2017, there were loads
of comments on websites, forums, and social

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:44.000
media suggesting that a better title would
have been "Ralph Wrecks the Internet" as that

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.120
better reflects the original film and its
title character.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.290
In fact, it seems that the online laments
about the title were so loud that they reached

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:55.800
the Wreck-It Ralph team at Disney who decided
to have a bit of light-hearted fun by mocking

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.990
both themselves and their fans in the movie's
trailer where Ralph asks the question on everyone's

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:01.990
lips.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.350
"Shouldn't it be Ralph Wrecks The Internet?"

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.560
"Yes, since he is Wreck-It Ralph."

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.979
"Ah, yeah, but 'Break The Internet', it's
like a 'thing'!"

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:12.480
"Right, it's just 'wreck the internet kinda
sounds better', doesn't it?"

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.120
"Hmm, you're not wrong."

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:17.720
Apparently, the filmmakers did originally
consider calling the film Ralph Wrecks The

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:22.190
Internet, but ended up going with "Breaks
The Internet" for the same reason Yesss gives:

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.960
because that's the expression!

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.490
Pixar fans will doubtless have enjoyed seeing
many of their favourite characters and easter

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:32.751
eggs show up in Wreck-It Ralph 2
Indeed, Pixar gets its own section at Oh My

00:02:32.751 --> 00:02:37.910
Disney with the animation studio's signature
desk lamp and Luxo ball adorning the area.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:42.460
There are also cameos by EVE from WALL-E,
Arlo from The Good Dinosaur, and Buzz Lightyear

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.730
is there meeting and greeting fans.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:49.750
And Pixar's Brave princess Merida also features
in the Disney Princess line-up when Vanellope

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.320
breaks into the restricted backstage area.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.320
However, the Disney animators couldn't help
themselves from throwing a little bit of shade

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.150
at Pixar and its fans in that scene.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:01.790
"I gave my Mammy a cake, she turned into a
big bear, my auld yin tried to do her in."

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.660
"If that's no a pure mess I don't know what
is."

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:05.660
"Eh."

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:06.660
"We can't understand her."

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:07.660
"She's from the other studio."

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:08.660
Apparently, the filmmakers did originally
consider calling the film Ralph Wrecks The

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:09.660
Internet, but ended up going with "Breaks
The Internet" for the same reason Yesss gives:

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:10.660
because that's the expression!

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.209
Now, the princess scene does poke fun at many
of the Disney princess tropes we've seen over

00:03:13.209 --> 00:03:18.250
the years, but Disney does seem to be having
a bit of an extra laugh at Merida and Pixar.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:22.500
I'm sure it's definitely got nothing to do
with the fact that Brave won the Oscar for

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:27.350
Best Animated Feature Film over Wreck-It Ralph
at the Academy Awards in 2013!

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:32.540
For anyone who found Merida's Scottish accent
difficult to follow in the trailer, well,

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:37.010
brace yourselves because Disney goes even
further in the actual movie as Merida's new

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:41.080
lines are delivered in an even thicker accent
so you're going to have an even tougher time

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.790
deciphering what she says in the final film.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:48.370
The entire Disney princess scene is, of course,
very funny, but some Pixar and Scottish fans

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:51.000
may feel a touch upset at Merida's treatment.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.800
By the way, if you'd like to check out all
the other Disney princess easter eggs in Ralph

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.670
Breaks The Internet, I'll add links to my
video on that at the end and in the video

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:00.670
description.

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:07.040
OK, spoilers ahead for the end credits which
are basically just jokes, so no story spoilers.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.590
If you think back to the first trailer Disney
released for Ralph Breaks The Internet, you'll

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:15.300
likely remember it ended with that poor exploding
bunny in the Pancake-Milkshake game.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.300
"Eat, eat!"

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:19.829
"Err, Ralph you might wanna try feeding the
kitty for a little while."

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:20.829
"No!

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:34.530
The kitty gets the milkshake, the bunny gets
the pancake..."

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:39.129
The filmmakers include a deliberate nod to
that horrifying trailer scene in the new film

00:04:39.129 --> 00:04:43.250
when Ralph makes a video unboxing a Pancake-Milkshake
fun set.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:48.280
However, by the end of the movie proper, there's
still no sign of any exploding bunnies; just

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:51.840
a few images of the game's cute cat and rabbit
in the fancy credits!

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.530
So, that trailer scene must have been cut
from the final film, you think to yourself!

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.479
But you're wrong!
Disney has actually been trolling you, and

00:04:59.479 --> 00:05:02.620
they reveal their master prank in a mid-credits
scene!

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:06.050
The scene starts with a mother asking her
daughter if she enjoyed the movie, and the

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:09.729
little girl replies,
"Weeellllll, there was a scene in the trailer

00:05:09.729 --> 00:05:11.439
that wasn't in the movie.

00:05:11.439 --> 00:05:12.809
And that makes me sad."

00:05:12.809 --> 00:05:17.990
So, the mum suggests the little girl play
a game to cheer up, and that game is – yes,

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:20.520
you guessed it – Pancake-Milkshake!

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:24.930
Cue the previously missing trailer segment
with Ralph and Vanellope force-feeding pancakes

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:29.370
to a helpless bunny in a scene reminiscent
of Mr Creosote in Monty Python's The Meaning

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:33.309
of Life and Violet Beauregarde in Willy Wonka
And The Chocolate Factory!

00:05:33.309 --> 00:05:37.789
The Wreck-It Ralph sequel team were obviously
having a bit of fun with their fans, especially

00:05:37.789 --> 00:05:42.320
the kind of movie lovers – you know the
ones – who enjoy finding out all about scenes

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:46.809
or characters deleted from movies as well
as alternate endings and any big story changes

00:05:46.809 --> 00:05:48.870
during the filmmaking process!

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:50.970
Well played, Disney, well played!

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:56.570
If you stick around till the very end of the
credits, Disney has got another rick-diculous

00:05:56.570 --> 00:06:00.460
surprise for you that ties in perfectly with
the movie's online setting.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:06.050
Yes, after all the credits have rolled, a
voice announces, 'And now, an exclusive sneak

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:08.139
peek of Frozen 2!'

00:06:08.139 --> 00:06:11.360
But if you think your patience is going to
be rewarded with a special glimpse at the

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.339
Frozen sequel, then think again because the
voice also tells you to 'click here', and

00:06:16.339 --> 00:06:20.490
although you'll hear some brief orchestral
music, the screen quickly changes to Ralph

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:24.889
singing and dancing along to the chorus of
Rick Astley's 80s hit Never Gonna Give You

00:06:24.889 --> 00:06:28.779
Up, complete with back-up dancing from Fix-It
Felix and Shank's crew!

00:06:28.779 --> 00:06:33.550
Yes, that's right, Disney just performed that
classic pranky online bait-and-switch, the

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:34.800
rick-roll, on us all!

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:40.030
And, as if Ralph rick-rolling us wasn't enough,
the video-game bad guy also talks directly

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:41.220
to the audience.

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:44.699
The way Ralph breaks the fourth wall in this
post-credits scene and tells us there’s

00:06:44.699 --> 00:06:48.419
really no reason to stick around in the cinema
any longer is a nice homage to John Hughes’s

00:06:48.419 --> 00:06:49.419
classic 80s comedy Ferris Bueller’s Day
Off, which was also received a tribute in

00:06:49.419 --> 00:06:50.419
the first Deadpool movie
The way Ralph breaks the fourth wall in this

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:51.419
post-credits scene and tells us there's really
no reason to stay is a nice homage to John

00:06:51.419 --> 00:06:53.669
Hughes's classic 80s comedy Ferris Bueller's
Day Off, which also received a tribute in

00:06:53.669 --> 00:06:54.810
the first Deadpool movie.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:59.050
Of course, this isn't the first time a character
in the Disney stable has trolled those of

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:03.089
us who stay right through the end credits
– I'm looking at you Captain America in

00:07:03.089 --> 00:07:04.229
Spider-Man: Homecoming!

00:07:04.229 --> 00:07:06.220
"Sometimes patience is the key to victory."

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:08.650
"Sometimes it leads to very little."

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:09.939
"It seems like it's not worth it."

00:07:09.939 --> 00:07:14.449
"And you wonder why you waited so long for
something so disappointing."

